retty consistent across head  height but there's a shift up for men  and these by the way this these are just  made up numbers nobody's like 500  centimeters tall and are in our data I  just wanted to show there's different  shapes that we were interested in and so  the graph on the right if that's the  true relationship is an indication that  this change is an evolutionary change  and so women and men have evolved  differently but now we actually see you  know something pretty consistent within  our different measurements and so for me  from a statistical perspective this was  a really interesting thing to fit  because on the right you know first I  tried to do some convincing that we  didn't really need to fit that model  because I wasn't really sure what to do  with it and then I realized well we can  just turn it into an additive error  model and let's just try and see maybe  if we have normally distributed errors  and  still in the middle of this analysis but  I don't want to give you the punchline  so our data here came actually from  basic trainees and so we have army basic  trainees at Fort Jackson South Carolina  that's most of our support branches go  through Fort Jackson all Air Force  initial entry training occurs at  Lackland Air Force Base so we have these  human solutions 3d body scanners we've  got almost 10,000 soldiers at Fort  Jackson and over 65,000 at Lackland and  one of the nice things here is you know  there's nowhere near 1/3 of the army our  feet of female soldiers but all of 60%  of female soldiers do their initial  entry training at Fort Jackson and so  we've got about a third of our sample  that's actually female and so you know  we just write up a little nice little  likelihood function we use the optimum  to try to actually maximize or minimize  the negative log likelihood because that  seem to be a little easier the only  trick here is to kind of have to have  good starting values but this function  is gonna solve numerically so if yo